        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "41fc903c-0c8b-3b32-a95d-14cd9ff86021",
                "word": "homérique",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "b1cbab40-1be2-42f3-96a8-20015a97437a",
                        "entry_id": "41fc903c-0c8b-3b32-a95d-14cd9ff86021",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "masculine",
                            "feminine"
                        ],
                        "form": "homériques"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d5c09def-8aac-3136-8553-fe2273a89507",
                        "entry_id": "41fc903c-0c8b-3b32-a95d-14cd9ff86021",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Qui appartient à Homère."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c58b700e-fb4f-4296-b3f2-db13b28b27db",
                                "sense_id": "d5c09def-8aac-3136-8553-fe2273a89507",
                                "text": "D’ailleurs... ce serait sensationnel si je mourais quand même à Pendragon. La prophétie s’accomplirait. J’entrerais dans la légende comme mes ancêtres qui ont vécu au bon vieux temps. Je serais comme un héros homérique dont la mort est toujours annoncées trois chant à l’avance. Sensationnel."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "00dd91f1-68b7-3c9d-8f44-4c962413174e",
                        "entry_id": "41fc903c-0c8b-3b32-a95d-14cd9ff86021",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Qui appartient à Homère.",
                            "Qualifie les hymnes attribués à Homère."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "especially"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "920d55e3-8ace-329b-a345-76b6d16faa7a",
                        "entry_id": "41fc903c-0c8b-3b32-a95d-14cd9ff86021",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Qui appartient à Homère.",
                            "Qualifie un style littéraire qui rappelle celui d’Homère."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "especially"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "431923ab-2fd6-35ba-851a-f0c163da2099",
                        "entry_id": "41fc903c-0c8b-3b32-a95d-14cd9ff86021",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Qualifie une situation digne des épisodes décrits par Homère."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "945a877e-3a94-4371-be83-73500f0c70ef",
                                "sense_id": "431923ab-2fd6-35ba-851a-f0c163da2099",
                                "text": "Il les récitait volontiers au milieu de joyeux groupes, […] pendant ces interminables « laaous, » festins homériques par lesquels se terminaient toutes les upa-upa ;[…]."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ca994aad-a277-4d9c-aa68-9cf3c7f8c8f4",
                                "sense_id": "431923ab-2fd6-35ba-851a-f0c163da2099",
                                "text": "J’offris le bras à la maîtresse de la maison, et l’on passa dans la salle à manger, où sur une table immense était servi un repas homérique dont le fumet appétissant me rappela que depuis près de douze heures j’étais à jeun."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9a283578-1294-4631-9f82-081b08f77dd4",
                                "sense_id": "431923ab-2fd6-35ba-851a-f0c163da2099",
                                "text": "[…]; les utopies sociales furent faites en vue de maintenir un noyau de guerriers homériques dans les cités, etc."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "86d3a892-c30e-49e7-be95-292b2f1c2aa0",
                                "sense_id": "431923ab-2fd6-35ba-851a-f0c163da2099",
                                "text": "Mais ce village, en temps de “guerre contre l’ennemi invisible” me rappelle plutôt le film La Grande Vadrouille, dont plusieurs scènes ont été tournées sur place – Tintin, le fossoyeur du village aujourd’hui décédé, racontait merveilleusement ce tournage homérique."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0f4118b9-abfc-3c5f-9022-1238eeecf7d0",
                        "entry_id": "41fc903c-0c8b-3b32-a95d-14cd9ff86021",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Qualifie un rire bruyant et de bon cœur, ainsi appelé à cause d’une scène du premier livre de l’Iliade, où les dieux éclatent de rire en voyant la démarche boiteuse d’Héphaïstos."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "especially"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "97d99ad0-7aec-4509-b717-09a880ba73e9",
                        "source_entry_id": "41fc903c-0c8b-3b32-a95d-14cd9ff86021",
                        "target_lang_word": "Homeric",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Homeric",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "458c6a39-37ae-3e50-8b65-40ad18c6511f",
                                        "entry_id": "3c21bfce-1424-39fc-8058-dee498e6d886",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Resembling or relating to the epic poetry of Homer."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "918ac71e-7634-4315-b760-81b340879a81",
                                                "sense_id": "458c6a39-37ae-3e50-8b65-40ad18c6511f",
                                                "text": "The warmeſt admirers of the great Mantuan poet [Virgil] can extol him for little more than the ſkill with which he has, by making his hero both a traveller and a warrior, united the beauties of the Iliad and the Odyſſey in one compoſition: yet his judgment was perhaps ſometimes overborne, by his avarice of the Homeric treaſures; and, for fear of ſuffering a ſparkling ornament to be loſt, he has inſerted it where it cannot ſhine with its original ſplendor."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0de51eb7-fc1e-4acd-acd6-73134d428658",
                                                "sense_id": "458c6a39-37ae-3e50-8b65-40ad18c6511f",
                                                "text": "In the beginning of the Fourth Book the poet [John Milton] introduces an Homerick cluster of similes; which seems to mark an intention of bestowing more poetical decoration on the conclusion of the Poem, than on the preceding parts of it."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "15b474ea-82ca-4f9a-a385-3b824c147f78",
                                                "sense_id": "458c6a39-37ae-3e50-8b65-40ad18c6511f",
                                                "text": "We, having obtained knowledge of the early derivation and distribution of mankind, and of the primitive religion, from sources other than those open to Homer, shall find in this knowledge the lost counterpart of a great portion of the Homeric myths. The theological and Messianic traditions which we find recorded in Scripture, when compared with the Homeric theogony, will be found to correspond with a large and important part of it: […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7e09bd2d-bd57-4bf8-8f31-eb7102d5d284",
                                                "sense_id": "458c6a39-37ae-3e50-8b65-40ad18c6511f",
                                                "text": "In some respects Homeric society is clearly an artificial literary creation. It is a natural tendency of all heroic epic to exaggerate the social status and behaviour of everyone involved, so that characters appear generally to belong to the highest social class and to possess great wealth and extraordinary abilities, in implicit contrast with the inequalities and squalor of the present age."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "32d1639a-fb61-382e-90d2-fde64098e1af",
                                        "entry_id": "3c21bfce-1424-39fc-8058-dee498e6d886",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or pertaining to Greece during the Bronze Age, as described in Homer's works."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f126f574-498e-4d1b-8e5f-6df850ae636d",
                                                "sense_id": "32d1639a-fb61-382e-90d2-fde64098e1af",
                                                "text": "Women are often carried away by force; and the whole tribe, as in Homeric times, rise to avenge the wrong."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6952a997-22c9-3742-b077-801448c0933b",
                                        "entry_id": "3c21bfce-1424-39fc-8058-dee498e6d886",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Fit to be immortalized in poetry by Homer; epic, heroic."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1b0b5cea-5cef-4f69-9dca-9e61a19a8e9b",
                                                "sense_id": "6952a997-22c9-3742-b077-801448c0933b",
                                                "text": "At home he [John Ruskin] looks young & rompish at the meeting, at Hunts meeting he looked old & ungainly, but his power & eloquence as a speaker were homeric."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "945a401c-aaef-4555-882e-1d62b0a4f9bb",
                                                "sense_id": "6952a997-22c9-3742-b077-801448c0933b",
                                                "text": "In San Francisco there was a barroom for every one hundred inhabitants. Here beer and bourbon fought a homeric battle for the hearts and throats of the thin line of patriots who balanced on the city's brass rails and voluntarily gave the national debt a lift."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "643ae999-7362-4bb7-a41a-c1ed91152854",
                        "source_entry_id": "41fc903c-0c8b-3b32-a95d-14cd9ff86021",
